%I A398795 #6 Aug 28 2026 22:06:27
%S A398795 0,0,0,1,2,5,11,23,48,99,203,413,838,1694,3419,6881,13843,27811,55818,
%T A398795 111980,224511,449933,901415,1805400,3615145,7237666,14487753,
%U A398795 28996767,58029789
%N A398795 Number of values 0 <= r < 2^n such that the trajectories of r and r+1 under the map f(x) = x/2 for x even, (7*x+1)/2 for x odd, satisfy f^(n)(r) = f^(n)(r+1) and contain the same number of odd terms among the first n iterates.
%C A398795 The analog of A398792 for the 7x+1 map; see A398794 for why the count is well defined without any convergence assumption. The density a(n)/2^n at n = 22 is 0.107, against 0.409 for the Collatz map (A398792) and 0.0044 for 5x+1 (A398794); it is nondecreasing in n and grows slowly on the computed range.
%C A398795 For maps (q*x+r)/2 with an odd prime p dividing r but not q, the corresponding count is 0 for every n: mod p the map is multiplication by units (2^-1 or 2^-1*q), and merging at equal step and odd counts equalizes the accumulated factors, forcing r == r+1 (mod p), which is impossible. (Tested on (5x+3)/2, (7x+3)/2 and (3x+5)/2: all identically zero.)
%C A398795 Relation to the 3x+1 problem: comparing this count with A398792 and A398794 singles out q = 3 as the only map in the family (q*x+1)/2 for which the difference of a consecutive pair neither grows nor shrinks on average, the expected multiplier being (1+q)/4. The three counts are not ordered by q: q = 7 gives a larger density than q = 5. Like A398792, this is not evidence for or against the Collatz conjecture.
%H A398795 Omar Said, <a href="https://github.com/theswordofthemorning/zootheorem">Statements, proofs, sequence data and verification scripts</a>
%e A398795 a(4) = 1: the residue r = 8 satisfies f^(4)(8) = f^(4)(9) = 4 with one odd step on each side (8 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1 -> 4 and 9 -> 32 -> 16 -> 8 -> 4).
%o A398795 (Python)
%o A398795 def a(n):
%o A398795     c = 0
%o A398795     for r in range(2**n):
%o A398795         x, y, sx, sy = r, r+1, 0, 0
%o A398795         for _ in range(n):
%o A398795             sx += x & 1; sy += y & 1
%o A398795             x = (7*x+1)//2 if x & 1 else x//2
%o A398795             y = (7*y+1)//2 if y & 1 else y//2
%o A398795             if x == y:
%o A398795                 c += sx == sy
%o A398795                 break
%o A398795     return c
%Y A398795 Cf. A185452, A398792, A398793, A398794.
%K A398795 nonn,more,new
%O A398795 1,5
%A A398795 _Omar Said_, Aug 23 2026
%E A398795 a(23)-a(29) from _Sean A. Irvine_, Aug 28 2026
